            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

There are some intentional holes in your house that would be eliminated if you upgrade the furnace and hot water tank to high efficiency, direct vent, condensing systems. The current chimney would be eliminated, as would the 6" open combustion air supply pipe. Although the current furnace &amp; hot water tank are in an enclosed room in the centre of the house, the doors do not seal and have a gap at the bottom ......... so air leakage does occur from these two intentional openings.



Eliminating these two intentional holes (as a result of upgrading tthe furnace and hot water tank) should decrease the current air leakage by a minimum 10%. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 45&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house has a John Wood JWF 307 oil hot water tank that is co-vented together with the furnace up the chimney. This hot water tank has a default energy factor of 0.60. As outlined in my furnace upgrade recommendation, I am looking to increase the efficiency of both the furnace and the hot water tank, while eliminating the need for the chimney. I would recommend you consider replacing this tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 218&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.4GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil furnace was originally rated at 82.1% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), and it vents up a 5" chimney, that is co-vented together with the 6" chimney from the hot water tank. There is a 6" open combustion air supply for the furnace &amp; hot water tank. There is also a 5" fresh air supply connected to the return air plenum of the furnace, that brings fresh air into the house every time the furnace operates. This fresh air is also part of the central ventilation system - the fantech exhaust fan in the lower level that connects the bathroom fan and the kitchen fan, and it interconnected to the furnace through the humidistat on the wall.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This furnace will draw its' combustion air directly from outside. Later in this report, you will see an upgrade recommendation for the hot water tank - and upgrading both appliances will allow you to completely eliminate the current chimney and the 6" open combustion air pipe. The 5" fresh air supply pipe (connected to the return air plenum of the furnace) &amp; the central ventilation system will remain.



As your oil tank is an older, single wall unit that is starting to show some rust, this may be a good time for you to consider a propane heating &amp; hot water system.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 760&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;15.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------









</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Using Biomass - Wood Stove &lt;/b&gt;



You currently have a wood stove on the main floor, that has a combustion air supply through the floor right beside it. I have estimated the annual savings for the following:



1. If you use your wood stove to offset 50% of the heating load requirement of the upper floor (compared to the current 82.1% AFUE oil furnace), the estimated annual savings are $1,055. This assumes a $100 per cord cost for self-harvested wood, compared to the cost of oil (outlined in the chart a little further down in this section).



2. If you use your wood stove to offset 50% of the heating load requirement of the upper floor (compared to the new 95% AFUE propane furnace), the estimated annual savings are $1,470. These savings include the $760 of savings associated with upgrading the current oil furnace to the high efficiency propane furnace - ie: there would be an additional $710 of savings for heating the upstairs with wood. This assumes a $100 per cord cost for self-harvested wood, compared to the cost of propane (outlined in the chart a little further down in this section).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has the following ventilation:



1. The kitchen range hood fan that vents to the outside.

2. The central ventilation system in the lower level (Fantech inline fan) that exhausts air from both the kitchen &amp; bathroom (using a wall switch in each room) and this fan is also interconnected via the humidistat on the wall that turns on both the Fantech fan &amp; the furnace fan. There is a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace that will bring fresh air into the house whenever the furnace is running or the Fantech inline fan is operational.

3. The clothes dryer vents to the outside.



It sounds like you are not really using the central ventilation system at all. I would suggest you connect with the Housing Corp. maintenance folks to have them provide instruction on the best way to use this system, including the summer switch that is on the wall just to the left side of the furnace. If the current 5" fresh air supply does not have a manual damper, it should have one installed, so you can adjust the amount of fresh air coming into the house.



As the current furnace appears to be the original, you will want your heating contractor (and the Housing Corp.) to confirm how a new furnace (either oil or propane) will interconnect with the current ventilation system, or if some changes will be required.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, the central ventilation fan (for the bathroom &amp; kitchen), and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.2 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace and hot water tank co-vent up a chimney, and has a 6" combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room. The current woodstove also vents up a chimney, and there is a combustion supply pipe at floor level right beside it.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in the house. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.70 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.07 per litre 

Propane - $0.60 per litre

Wood - $367.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.35 per bag, $393.75 per tonne bulk delivered



